export const hints: string[] = [
  "# Chrome (com.android.chrome)\n\n- Address bar at the TOP by default (on some setups it's at the bottom): node with text \"Search or type web address\" (desc/resource \"url_bar\"), or the current URL when a page is open.\n- Right of the address bar: \"Switch or close tabs\" (tab count), \"Customize and control Google Chrome\" (three dots menu). New Tab page shows a Google search box and shortcut tiles.\n- **Search the web / open a site**: tap the address bar → `type` the query or URL → key `enter`.\n- **Results**: Google results are a web view. Links usually expose their title text; tap the result title.\n- Back: key `back` goes to the previous page. Menu → \"New tab\", \"Bookmarks\", \"History\".\n\nWhat goes wrong:\n- Web pages are web views: many buttons have no label, only nearby text. Prefer tapping visible link text; if nothing matches, ask for a fresh `screen` with an image or scroll.\n- Cookie/consent banners (\"Accept all\", \"Reject all\", \"I agree\") cover content. Choose \"Reject all\" when available, else \"Accept all\" only if needed to continue.\n- \"Open in app?\" or \"Sign in to Chrome\" prompts: tap \"No thanks\"/\"Stay in Chrome\"/\"Use without an account\".\n- The address bar hides when scrolling down. Scroll up slightly (swipe `down`) to bring it back.\n- The keyboard covers the lower half of forms; press `back` once to hide it (a second `back` leaves the page).\n- Typing into a focused field the page owns may be refused; tap the field first.\n",
  "# Gmail (com.google.android.gm)\n\n- **Inbox:** the top bar is a search field labelled \"Search in mail\". To its left is \"Open navigation drawer\". To its right is the account picture (\"Account and settings\"). Bottom right is a big \"Compose\" button.\n- **Email rows:** each row's text or description holds the sender, subject and preview, often all in one (\"Sarah Chen, Running late, can we push to 3?, Unread\"). The newest email is at the top. To open one, tap it by the sender's name, such as `{\"text\":\"Sarah Chen\"}`. Text matching also finds partial matches, so the name alone is enough.\n- **Open email:** the sender's name and the subject are near the top. Before replying, check that the sender is the person the user named. At the bottom of the message are \"Reply\", \"Reply all\" and \"Forward\". On a long email you may need to `swipe up` to reach them. Top right are \"Archive\", \"Delete\", \"Mark unread\" and \"More options\".\n- **Reply:** open the email → tap \"Reply\" → the message body (hint \"Compose email\") gets the focus → `type` the text with no `on` → tap \"Send\" (the paper plane at the top right; its description is \"Send\").\n- **New email:** tap \"Compose\" → in the \"To\" field, type a name or address, then tap the suggested contact → tap \"Subject\" and type → tap \"Compose email\" and type → tap \"Send\".\n- **Search:** tap \"Search in mail\" → type the search → `key enter` → the results list.\n\n## What goes wrong\n\n- The keyboard can hide \"Reply\", \"Forward\" and the bottom of the body. \"Send\" is always at the top, so it stays reachable. If you need something under the keyboard, `key back` once to hide it.\n- An email that's part of a conversation opens the whole thread. \"Reply\" answers the last message.\n- Suggested-reply buttons (\"Sounds good!\", \"Thanks!\") sit above \"Reply\". Don't tap them unless one says exactly what the user wants.\n- A tab bar (\"Mail\", \"Meet\") may show at the bottom. Stay on \"Mail\".\n- Several accounts: the account picture switches between them. Check you're in the right inbox before searching.\n- After sending, a \"Sent\" message with \"Undo\" shows for a few seconds. Never tap \"Undo\".\n- `key back` from an unsent email can ask \"Discard draft?\" or save a draft. Only discard if the user cancelled. Never leave drafts behind.\n",
  "# Samsung home screen (One UI launcher)\n\nThe screen's app is `com.sec.android.app.launcher`. You see it after `key home`.\n\n- **App icons** show up as nodes whose `text` is the app's name under the icon, such as \"Gmail\", \"Messages\" or \"Chrome\". They are tappable. Tap the label to open the app.\n- **The dock** is the row at the very bottom (high `top` values, around 2000 to 2300 on a 2400-pixel-tall screen). It holds the same 4 or 5 apps on every page, usually Phone, Messages, Internet or Chrome, and Camera. Check it first.\n- **Pages:** the home screen has several pages side by side. To see the next page, `swipe left`. To go back a page, `swipe right`. After each swipe, read the screen again. If the labels didn't change, you're on the last page.\n- **Page dots:** a small row of dots above the dock shows which page you're on. It may have a description such as \"Page 2 of 3\". Use it to know how many pages are left.\n- **Folders** look like an app icon with a name such as \"Google\" or \"Social\". Gmail is often inside a folder named \"Google\". Tap the folder name to open it. Its apps then show up as nodes over the home screen. Tap the app inside. If the app isn't there, `key back` closes the folder.\n- **App drawer** (every installed app, A to Z): from the home screen, `swipe up` from the middle of the screen. It has a search field at the top (\"Search\" or \"Search phone\"). Use it as a fallback when the app isn't on any home page.\n- **Widgets** (clock, weather, Google search bar) also show up as nodes. Ignore them. Tapping the Google search bar opens a search, not an app.\n\n## What goes wrong\n\n- A swipe that starts on a widget can scroll the widget instead of changing the page. If the screen didn't change, swipe again from another spot, such as `{\"x\":540,\"y\":1500}`.\n- `swipe down` on the home screen opens the notification shade. `key back` closes it.\n- A long press opens an edit menu (\"Remove\", \"Select\", \"Edit\"). If you see those words, `key back`.\n- Labels can be cut short (\"Messa…\"). Match on the start of the name.\n- If the screen shows a lock screen, or anything that isn't the launcher after `key home`, say so. Don't try to unlock it.\n",
  "# Google Maps (com.google.android.apps.maps)\n\n- Main screen: search box at the top, text \"Search here\"; right of it \"Voice search\" and the account avatar. Category chips below (\"Restaurants\", \"Coffee\", \"Gas\", …). Bottom tabs: \"Explore\", \"You\", \"Contribute\". A \"Directions\"/\"Go\" button may float bottom right; \"Re-centre\"/\"Your location\" button above it.\n- **Search a place**: tap \"Search here\" → `type` the query (e.g. \"coffee near me\") → key `enter` (or tap the first suggestion row) → results list slides up from the bottom.\n- Results: each row has the place name, rating, distance, open/closed; buttons per row \"Directions\", \"Call\", \"Website\", \"Save\" (some only on the place page).\n- **Open a place**: tap its name → place sheet with \"Directions\", \"Start\", \"Call\", \"Save\", \"Share\".\n- **Directions**: place sheet → \"Directions\" → choose mode tab (\"Driving\", \"Transit\", \"Walking\") → \"Start\". Only tap \"Start\" if the user asked to navigate.\n\nWhat goes wrong:\n- The map itself is one big unlabelled view. Never tap the map; work with the search box, chips and the results list.\n- The results sheet is half-height; swipe `up` on it to see more rows, `down` to show the map.\n- Location permission/\"Turn on location accuracy\" dialogs: tap \"Only this time\"/\"While using the app\" or \"No thanks\"; never change system settings.\n- Sponsored results (\"Ad\") appear first; prefer the first non-ad result unless the user named a place.\n- After searching, `back` clears results; a second `back` leaves Maps.\n",
  "# Google Messages (com.google.android.apps.messaging) / Samsung Messages (com.samsung.android.messaging)\n\nThe S21 may use either app. Check `app` in the screen result.\n\n**Google Messages**\n- Conversation list: search bar at top (\"Search conversations\" or \"Search images & videos\"), avatar on the right. Bottom right: \"Start chat\" FAB.\n- Rows show the contact name and the last message. Tap the name to open.\n- In a conversation: text field at the bottom with hint \"Text message\" (or \"RCS message\"/\"Chat message\"), \"Send SMS\"/\"Send message\" button to its right (appears after typing; before typing it's a voice-message mic).\n- **Send a text to an existing contact**: tap the contact's row → tap the message field → `type` → tap the send button (desc contains \"Send\").\n- **New conversation**: \"Start chat\" → \"To\" field (\"Type a name, phone number, or email\") → type the name → tap the matching contact → type in the message field → send.\n\n**Samsung Messages**\n- Compose button bottom right, desc \"Compose new message\" (pencil). Search is the magnifier (\"Search\") at top.\n- In a thread: field \"Enter message\", send button desc \"Send\".\n\nWhat goes wrong:\n- The keyboard covers the conversation but the send button stays above it. Don't press `back` before sending; it may close the thread.\n- Contacts with several numbers show a picker. Choose \"Mobile\" if unsure, or ask.\n- \"Set as default SMS app?\" dialogs: dismiss with \"Cancel\"/\"Not now\", never change defaults.\n- RCS/chat features banners (\"Turn on chat features\"): ignore or tap \"Not now\".\n",
  "# Settings (com.android.settings, Samsung One UI)\n\n- Top: big title \"Settings\", then a search icon (\"Search\") and the Samsung account row. List of sections: \"Connections\", \"Connected devices\", \"Modes and Routines\", \"Sounds and vibration\", \"Notifications\", \"Display\", \"Battery\", \"Wallpaper and style\", \"Themes\", \"Home screen\", \"Lock screen\", \"Security and privacy\", \"Location\", \"Safety and emergency\", \"Accounts and backup\", \"Google\", \"Advanced features\", \"Digital Wellbeing and parental controls\", \"Device care\", \"Apps\", \"General management\", \"Accessibility\", \"Software update\", \"About phone\".\n- **Fastest route**: tap \"Search\" (magnifier) → `type` the setting name (e.g. \"dark mode\", \"Bluetooth\") → tap the matching result.\n- Toggles: a row's text is the setting name; the switch is often a separate node with the same text or desc \"On\"/\"Off\". Tap the switch, or the row (some rows open a sub-page instead).\n- Common paths: Wi-Fi/Bluetooth/Airplane mode → \"Connections\". Dark mode → \"Display\" → \"Dark\". Brightness → \"Display\". Volume → \"Sounds and vibration\". Do not disturb → \"Notifications\" → \"Do not disturb\" (or Modes and Routines on newer One UI).\n\nWhat goes wrong:\n- Settings changes affect the real phone and can cut the hub connection. Never turn off Wi-Fi, mobile data, Bluetooth (earbuds!), accessibility services, or Bobby's notification access unless the user explicitly asked; confirm first for those.\n- Long lists: swipe `up` to scroll. Section names differ between One UI versions; use Search if a name isn't visible.\n- Quick Settings (pull-down panel, key `notifications`, then swipe `down` again) is faster for Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Flashlight, Do not disturb; tiles show \"Wi-Fi, On\" style descriptions.\n- Some pages ask for PIN/biometrics. Stop and tell the user; never guess.\n",
  "# Spotify (com.spotify.music)\n\n- Bottom tabs: \"Home\", \"Search\", \"Your Library\", sometimes \"Premium\"/\"Create\".\n- **Search**: tap \"Search\" tab → tap the field \"What do you want to listen to?\" → `type` → results with filter chips (\"Songs\", \"Artists\", \"Playlists\", \"Albums\", \"Podcasts\").\n- Result rows: title + type (\"Song • Artist\"). Tapping a song row plays it (on Free it may shuffle the artist/playlist instead).\n- Artist/playlist/album pages: big green \"Play\" button (desc \"Play\" / \"Play playlist\"), \"Shuffle\", \"Follow\"/\"Save to Your Library\".\n- Mini-player above the bottom tabs: track name, \"Play\"/\"Pause\", \"Connect to a device\". Tap the track name to open Now Playing with \"Pause\", \"Next\", \"Previous\", \"Shuffle\", \"Repeat\", \"Like\".\n\nCommon tasks:\n- **Play an artist/song**: Search → type name → tap the top result → \"Play\".\n- **Pause**: tap \"Pause\" in the mini-player.\n\nWhat goes wrong:\n- Free accounts show ads and \"Upgrade to Premium\" cards. Dismiss with \"Dismiss\"/\"Not now\"/\"×\" (\"Close\").\n- \"Connect to a device\" can cast to speakers; avoid it.\n- Labels on Home cards are often long combined descriptions; match on the artist/playlist name.\n- If audio routes to earbuds already used by Voice, mention it rather than changing devices.\n",
  "# WhatsApp (com.whatsapp)\n\n- Home: tabs along the bottom: \"Chats\", \"Updates\", \"Communities\", \"Calls\". Top: \"WhatsApp\" title, \"Camera\", \"Search\" (magnifier) or a search bar \"Ask Meta AI or Search\", \"More options\".\n- Chat list rows: contact/group name + last message + time. Tap the name to open.\n- New chat: green FAB bottom right, desc \"New chat\". Then the contact list has \"Search name or number…\" at the top.\n- In a chat: message field at the bottom with hint \"Message\"; left \"Emoji\", right \"Attach\", \"Camera\". Once text is typed, the mic turns into \"Send\".\n\nCommon tasks:\n- **Send a message**: Chats → tap the contact's name (or \"Search\" → type the name → tap the result) → tap \"Message\" → `type` → tap \"Send\".\n- **Reply to a specific message**: long-press the message → \"Reply\" icon in the top bar → type → \"Send\". Usually a normal message is enough.\n\nWhat goes wrong:\n- Avoid the \"Meta AI\" entry in search; it's a chatbot, not a contact.\n- Before typing, the right-hand button is \"Voice message\"; tapping it starts a recording. Only tap \"Send\" after text is in the field.\n- Video/voice call icons (\"Video call\", \"Voice call\") sit in the chat's top bar. Never tap them unless asked.\n- Backup/update/\"Allow notifications\" pop-ups: dismiss with \"Not now\"/\"Skip\"/\"Later\".\n- Group names and contacts can share first names. Confirm the exact name on screen.\n"
];
